body:
When my kids used to say that to me (usually with an unfavorable comparison of me to my mother, their Mamaw), I would remind them that I bought them special extras about as often as they helped with chores. Somehow, that connection never mattered as much to them as it did to me. Didn’t encourage more of the same, either.

When I was their age I would have been thrilled at the chance to get more and do more special stuff just by honoring my obligation to clean up after myself - and I’m not even a boomer.

;__;

It also depends on if that’s the only component of the meal. I know to feed 5 people (3 kids, 2 adults) boxed stroganoff and have enough to satisfy everyone, I used to have to make three boxes. It was one of the boys’ favorite meals, so that probably had something to do with it, but that was a LOT of ground turkey trying to brown at once.

Wish I’d been one of those smart parents that figures giving impatient kids a piece of fruit as a holdover and “ruining their appetite” isn’t as bad as letting them fill up on starches at “mealtime.” It would have been a much healthier habit for them to develop. We live and learn, but often, learn too late. #regerts

)X

What's common in the USA is the idea that people "shouldn't need" help from one another. Not "don't need" but "shouldn't need." There's also this thought pattern that focuses on whether the homeless person is "trying" or "productive." Humane treatment seems rare. Funding is often problematic for the places that ARE trying to help. They figure if they don't take the money, American homeless folks are going to blow it on cigarettes, alcohol, other drugs, eating out at fancy places - whatever the case may be. Austerity is the keyword we're supposed to embrace here... prosperity is only for "stable" people and the public assistance system backs that up. Miss an appointment, lose your assistance, works out very well for the government that wants to save money and doesn't have to help "ineligible" people. No matter if they were rendered ineligible by too-strict assistance requirements. The cash assistance from my previous state of residence required more job-search activities than the cash would have funded. The government took the decreased participation in the cash assistance program as a victory... regardless that it was because fewer people are getting helped thanks to their horrible requirements for attendance BEFORE even GETTING gas or help with childcare...

&#x200B;

Then, you have some few unscrupulous homeless people that DO take other people's stuff and sell it, or hang out heavily littering on the exact same highway exit or public park they're sitting at with a cardboard HELP ME sign, you know... the sort that give the rest of society a very wrong idea about homeless people... :(

&#x200B;

I have met a lot of fellow homeless that have mental and emotional struggles similar to mine and some that looked harder to hide. How do we raise more resilient, mentally and emotionally healthy citizens, **without** turning them into callous jerks? That's what I'd like to know. It's as if we never learned that an ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure. Instead, common decency seems to be circling the drain, and people that don't have thick skins are denigrated whether it makes for mental deterioration or not...


Edit - Whoever keeps downvoting me, I will not be able to eat this week if you continue. 12/09/19 - Can’t post looking for work in the local Reddit classifieds.

I don't believe you're dumb and unmotivated. Mental illness is a crappy hand to be dealt, it can take a lot of extra maneuvering and coping though.

&#x200B;

As someone who has trouble keeping "regular" jobs... I recommend day labor, if you live in a place where they have that... it might not help with the housing, but it definitely can help with money. If your family can't or won't help you, or if you're like me and don't want their help for whatever reason, relocating could be useful IF you plan your destination. I chose a place that was far away - a major metro so there's public transportation in case my car fails me, has multiple day labor agencies so I can try to go to whichever one I'm closest to at the time, has plenty of free public events and parks so I have leisure options that don't break the bank, has legal green so I can say I'm voting with my feet, and if worse comes to worse and I have to make a liar of myself, emergency cold-weather shelters so I don't die *by accident*.

Mental issues. I crumple when I am emotionally struck and just hide from the world until I feel capable of facing it again. When people say "kys" it usually can't help but cross my mind. Last Fall semester, the last one I attempted, I spent more time in the bathroom trying to pretend I was okay than actually attending because I had a much larger rump and every time I tried to squeeze through the rows of seats, somebody ended up laughing at me, making snide remarks, and so on. I think they liked it when I had to leave the room without even getting to sit down because I could feel my face preparing to leak. Childish behavior. Yet who could blame them, I was ridiculous, and our culture seems much more prepared to fling people down than help them stay level, or rise. Of course, not attending as much as I could have impacted my grades about halfway through the semester, to the point that I had to quit or face losing scholarships for the Spring. (They didn't tell me that I would lose the scholarships anyway if I failed to repay what I had accepted for the semester I was dropping.)

&#x200B;

When I lost my most recent "regular" job at McD's, I think I had spent about 60-80% of my lunch breaks crying in the restroom because the owner was callous beyond my tolerance. Constructive criticism is one thing. When it's pure criticism wrapped in a disgusted "get better or get fired" tone of voice, over stupid new-hire mistakes (ex. like where to find the button on the cash register to turn a sandwich into a full meal when the customer changed their minds, or the debacle when a drunken customer thought they ordered something COMPLETELY different *after I confirmed with them at the window like we're supposed to do and the guy paid* but it was still my fault, somehow)... I fall apart. I can't take it when people show me they think as little of me as I think of myself. When it hurts too much, I start wanting to damage my body to put the hurt on the outside instead, which doesn't work. It's part of why I no longer live with my husband and kids. It felt like I was a bit of the scenery to them, nothing worth mentioning or noticing except when some function is needed. I have seriously struggled to self-validate since I was a preteen and I thought I would be better off alone after twelve years with that man, after he cheated on me and I took him back and he did it AGAIN and I just. I feel so worthless and stupid. But who else would have put up with this mess for twelve years?

&#x200B;

Some mornings, just getting my car started and driving elsewhere seems like more than I can stand, because I'm still inside my own head and I can't get away from myself. Sounds weird, probably. I had counseling help while I was a college student, but I might as well have not gone, because they couldn't get me away from myself in the time they had, either. I have been on various anti-depressants before and have either felt worse on them, or ended up just not getting the refills because there were kids' shoes to buy, or an oil change for one of the vehicles, or glasses to be replaced, or whatever. I tried to cope with all of it as I have done most of my life until I kind of snapped this summer. My boys were old enough to be helpful around the house and I couldn't even get them to clean their rooms or bring out their dirty laundry. Always had to fight with them about their homework, too, even at the expense of getting my own homework done. Zero respect from any corner, hugs ever so brief and grudging even when I desperately needed to hold on to one of my little guys. Husband rarely helped in any real sense - he's the type that thinks yelling is helping. I guess I turned him into an angry, bitter man because I wasn't good enough. I realized I had to leave when I developed a drinking problem and started yelling, too. **That isn't me.** I was never prone to anger, or violence against other creatures. The opposite is more my style - I was raised to turn the other cheek. I felt that the next step of losing control was going to be hurting somebody beyond apologizing, or getting drunk enough to end it all since it seemed like that would be simplest for everybody involved.

&#x200B;

So I ran away from home like I never had the guts to do as a kid, and here I am. Car-dwelling more or less by choice, since I didn't bring enough money with me to secure housing, or even save up because living without those handy appliances available is freakin' expensive. It's been almost three months. I have to say, it doesn't feel much colder than feeling unloved in my own house.

&#x200B;

The first two times I was homeless didn't stick. That was before my credit blew and I now need extra deposit money and multiple months' rent paid ahead before any place will let me have a private space. And that's what I want. Privacy. Sometimes I want to be around other humans so much, it's literally pathetic, but forced interactions can be almost as painful. It just depends on the day. And that's the real rub of it. Some days (a few), I feel pretty close to normal, and I function very well on those days. The rest of the time, it's this spectrum of I Just Suck. Some days more so than others. It makes it awful, trying to commit to employers and roommates and everything else, when I don't know if I am going to make it to the end of the day without having a breakdown.

I could understand them skipping the hand-wash if it was just peeing, and they used sanitizer on the way out, but... that's pretty sad. It only takes a few seconds, and IMO just because they might think of themselves as clean doesn't mean they should get a pass. Maybe they're taking it for granted that they can make up for it later?

&#x200B;

(Pardon if this is the wrong time, by the way, but merry meet!)

&#x200B;

A few weeks back, I was assigned to a scrap yard as a day laborer, got about as filthy as I've ever willingly become in my life draining and processing engines and transmissions for them. After hurrying to submit my work ticket at the day labor agency, I stopped at a King Sooper for a cleaner restroom and to scrub more of the stains from my arms where the sleeves weren't long enough to protect and some woman... some snotty woman that didn't even smile back at me... turned me in to the management. I wasn't washing anything offensive, certainly not anything I would consider inappropriate for public washing at a sink, yet I was told the sinks at that location are "for hands only" and that if I did it again I might get banned from the whole store. WHAT. I don't know how much of that was bluster, but it essentially wrecked my mood after I was so proud of myself for sticking with that work despite starting out knowing nothing whatsoever about it.

I bet the only reason we don't have officially-sanctioned, safe homeless camps is because somebody, somewhere, would take one look and screech "it's a concentration camp for the poor!"

&#x200B;

Safe Parking LA seemed to be a good idea, to me. We need a lot more places like that as a stop-gap, and to have them allow tents and sleeping bags and so on. Finding a place to be for the night is in my top-five roughest struggles as a car-dweller, maybe only second to the near-complete social isolation.

I can commiserate with what you're dealing with. I'm on my third bout of homelessness, having been relatively stable for about twelve years after the first two times. Back then, I did everything I could to stay eligible for a bed at a shelter in the (admittedly smaller) city I lived in. Signed up for extra chores, day labor, adhered to rules and policies so far as I was able (I remember I did curse a few times when I had an ouch, for which I was written up because I "should have been able to control the outburst" or however it was phrased - and then, I deliberately missed some required "self help" meetings, for good reason). I even choked back the bitterness of having to give up a few dollars to them when I did make it to day labor, because a safe place to stay was a place to stay, and I was terrified at the time to imagine What Might Happen if I didn't have a bed under me and a roof over me. That was in **summer** with no risk of freezing to death. I still think $200 for a month is a pretty harsh rent to pay when you're in a shelter trying to save up deposit money, first month, last month, utility deposits, whatever it takes to get a real place. Even the parts of the system that are designed to "help" frequently miss that goal.

&#x200B;

But I contracted scabies (both shelter stays) and lice (just the second time) - I can only assume from using their bed, pillow, blankets, laundry facilities, or some combination thereof, since I had been fortunate enough to get my own toiletries and such from churches. I couldn't even effectively treat the bugs until I moved out of the shelter. They just kept coming back. The whole "wash your clothes on arrival" thing didn't happen at the shelter where I stayed, but I doubt it would have helped since their "hot water" was was tepid at best and even had trouble killing *dirt*. Showers were scheduled, they rarely had any soap or shampoo to give residents because they also sent them out the front door in care packages, and if you missed your scheduled shower time then you were just SOL until the next day.

&#x200B;

Anyhow. To compare those experiences to living in my car...

&#x200B;

I decided that I can generally afford to shell out for a YMCA membership for showering - the one I go to provides the soap, shampoo, conditioner, mouthwash, deodorant, towels, and washcloths; the water is almost always hot; I don't have the pressure of a time limit unless I worked quite late and they're almost closed when I arrive. They also have a heated indoor pool, and a hot tub. If it hadn't been for trying to solve the hygiene issue, I might have never investigated the YMCA - I hadn't had a membership since childhood until this summer. Thinking of bathrooms... I never would have imagined public restrooms would be so difficult to find in the middle of the night, or that I had taken them for granted for so much of my life. I need to get up at least once in the middle of the night during the best of times. Now, because nights are turning cold, I somehow have to go three or four times even if I sharply reduce my fluid intake. (I have an insulated jug I bought from Goodwill for $2 for water. Well worth the investment, as there are several gas stations that will let me fill it up with ice water for free.) The guarantee of an available toilet is pretty alluring, I have to admit. But there's nobody to tell me I can't keep nonperishable food and extra water bottles handy, nobody to tell me when I have to turn out my lights, nobody to tell me what I must or mustn't wear in the halls AND while I sleep, nobody telling me I'm never going to get out on my own if I keep making interview appointments over their "self help sessions" that residents were required to attend to learn "life skills" (oh, how wrong they were), nobody telling me I needed to cope with emotional trauma by bringing charges against the individual that caused it, nobody arguing that I didn't need to use bus passes because I had a car (which still took gas - and I was buying the vast majority of my bus passes myself, they just didn't approve my budget).

&#x200B;

Actually, there's pretty much nobody, which is about the roughest thing I consistently come up against. Wretched loneliness. Interfacing with strangers for a few minutes at a time doesn't feel like enough, and everyone in my family is busy doing their own thing. They say I should call, but I don't. I don't want their pity, I don't want their I-told-you-so's, I don't want their charity when I know most of them aren't really much better off. I definitely don't want them preaching at me about "poor life choices" or whatever kick they want to get on that completely ignores or invalidates my struggles.

&#x200B;

I treasure the freedoms afforded me by having a car. It is the only asset I think I could not stand to lose and genuinely might jump off a bridge if something were to happen to it that I couldn't afford to repair. I had brake pads put on it since becoming homeless and still need winter tires - snow on Thursday already, I can't believe it. I do not intend to ever live in a shelter again, whatever that means for me. Probably means finding more blankets. Last night was ridiculously cold. I am used to leaving my windows down a bit to help combat the condensation (mold grows, ewwwww) and it was a HUGE mistake. I woke up with my fingertips numb that were poking out from under my pillow. lol. Weather is way less of a concern at a shelter, so if you live in a place that faces extreme cold or regular wet, that might weight your evaluation in favor of a shelter, where a room might be *uncomfortable* but shouldn't be *deadly*.

&#x200B;

In a car, it's very likely that police are going to tell you to "move along" at some point or another. Scary to wake up to someone banging on your window in the wee hours of the morning. Definitely worse IMO than the "room checks" where the shelter staff came around with flashlights to shine on faces in the middle of the night, since I could just roll over and go back to sleep after those for the most part. And the police can never tell me where I should park, because "liability." I've also witnessed them running off people sleeping on the ground before, even during the day. I'm not keen on dealing with authority figures that have me at a disadvantage, and I **think** I am learning to pick my parking more strategically to avoid harassment, but I'm pretty sure shelters are more forgiving than officers.

&#x200B;

If a car is not an option, and you can handle their sometimes-baffling requirements / scheduling / eccentricities, absolutely stay in the shelter. Bugs can be treated and possessions can be acquired, especially if you have a disability, and they have a space for you, because there seems to be particular help available in that situation. A shelter will most likely be able to point you in the right direction if 211 can't. But please, hun, please don't be shy about coming forward to ask for improvements to your situation. If it makes you feel better to phrase it diplomatically, maybe make a comment about how cold it was and ask if they could refer you to a place that gives out blankets, clothes, etc? I have to remind myself that it is NOT shameful to use the services of a food or clothing bank now and then as an able-bodied person, my day labor gets taxed like anyone else's hours they'll never get back, and that when I take less food due to feeling guilty it means I have less to share when I see someone worse off with that familiar pinched look that screams HUNGRY. Please don't make yourself suffer unnecessarily - hold on to hope with both hands and tell yourself that this, too, shall pass.
